 In C programming, there are several ways to handle and return errors. One common approach is to use error codes, where a specific numeric value is returned to indicate an error occurred. Here's a simple example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int myFunction(int input) {
    if (input < 0) {
        return -1; // Error code for invalid input
    }

    // Perform some operations...

    return 0; // Success
}

int main() {
    int result = myFunction(-1);

    if (result == -1) {
        printf("Error: Invalid input\n");
    } else {
        printf("Function executed successfully\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Another approach is to use pointers to pass error messages back to the calling function. This can be useful when you need to provide more detailed information about the error.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int myFunction(char **error_message, int input) {
    if (input < 0) {
        *error_message = "Invalid input";
        return -1; // Error code for invalid input
    }

    // Perform some operations...

    *error_message = NULL; // No error occurred
    return 0; // Success
}

int main() {
    char *error_message = NULL;
    int result = myFunction(&error_message, -1);

    if (result == -1) {
        printf("Error: %s\n", error_message);
        free(error_message); // Free the memory allocated by myFunction
    } else {
        printf("Function executed successfully\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Both of these methods allow you to handle errors in your C code and provide meaningful feedback to the user or the calling function.

